#> ─── agentify template: skill.md.tmpl ─────────────────────────────────────────
#> Emits: .claude/skills/{{SKILL_NAME}}/SKILL.md        (Claude Code)
#>        .agents/skills/{{SKILL_NAME}}/SKILL.md          (Codex — committable; NOT `.codex/skills/`,
#>        which is not a load path). Same format and same `name` + `description` progressive
#>        disclosure on both, so this one template serves both targets and the body is identical.
#>        With both targets selected, write both files rather than symlinking, and never write a
#>        skill into a tree the user already has it in — `discovery` reports shared entries by
#>        resolved path. adapters/codex.md §4.4.
#>
#> Filling rules
#>  1. Replace every placeholder. A placeholder is an ALL_CAPS name inside double braces.
#>  2. Fill every token from discovery.json / signals.json. A token you can only fill by guessing
#>     means this candidate has no evidence — drop the candidate, do not invent content.
#>  3. Delete every line beginning with "#>" and every {{!-- ... --}} block before writing the file.
#>     Nothing marked that way may survive into the emitted skill.
#>  4. Lines marked "repeat/trim" are variable-length lists: duplicate or delete rows to match the
#>     real number of items. Never leave an unfilled row behind.
#>  5. Keep the five agentify-* lines verbatim. verify_artifacts.py parses them, and a rerun matches
#>     on agentify-id to rewrite this file in place instead of writing a second copy.
#>  6. Optional frontmatter key: add `allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash` only when the skill
#>     genuinely needs a narrower tool set than the session default. Omit the key otherwise.
#>  7. Target length after filling: 60-150 lines. Longer skills get skimmed, not followed. Push the
#>     detail into reference files (rule 9) rather than growing this file.
#>  8. No attribution anywhere in the body. The one comment line below is the only placement.
#>  9. REFERENCE FILES — how a skill gets deep without getting long. Put anything the skill needs
#>     only at one step into `references/<name>.md` beside this file, and link it from the step
#>     that needs it. The agent loads a reference only when it reaches that step, so the skill's
#>     resident cost stays small. The four that recur, and what each holds:
#>       references/conventions.md   — this repo's conventions for the thing the skill produces,
#>                                     read off the code: naming, file layout, the idioms the
#>                                     existing examples use. Cite the real files it was read from.
#>       references/context.md       — the context-gathering procedure: which files to read, which
#>                                     commands to run, and what question each one answers, BEFORE
#>                                     writing anything. This is what makes the output fit the repo.
#>       references/output-format.md — the exact shape of what the skill produces, with one filled
#>                                     example taken from this repo.
#>       references/guardrails.md    — what the skill must never do here, drawn from corrections[]
#>                                     and pain_signals[], in the developer's own words.
#>     Write a reference file only when it has real repo-specific content. Two thin ones are worse
#>     than none. Every reference file gets the same agentify metadata header as this file, in an
#>     HTML comment, and is listed in the build manifest as type `reference`.
#> 10. THE PERSONALIZATION CHECK — run it before writing the file, on the filled text.
#>     Five tests, from blueprint.md §1.1: does it name at least 3 concrete identifiers from this
#>     repo? does it state THIS repo's convention rather than a best practice? does its evidence
#>     line name a field and a number? **can you name a repo it would be false in?** does it answer
#>     something the developer actually hit? A skill that fails any of them is rewritten, not
#>     shipped. Test 4 is the sharp one: if the file would be equally true in an unrelated project,
#>     it is a template, and templates are the one thing this tool does not produce.
#> 11. EXISTING VENDORED SKILLS ARE REFERENCES, NOT COVERAGE. A third-party skill already in the
#>     repo (`provenance.vendored_artifacts`: a `drizzle-orm-patterns`, a `trigger-tasks`, a
#>     `neon-postgres`) is the vendor's API guide. Link it from the one step that needs that API
#>     detail — "for the query builder's syntax read .agents/skills/drizzle-orm-patterns/SKILL.md" —
#>     never copy it, never let it supply a step, and never treat its presence as a reason this
#>     skill is not built (blueprint.md §2.1). A user-scope skill is not linked at all: a teammate
#>     does not have it.
#> 12. WHERE THE NOUNS COME FROM. Phase 7 may read, per skill, the files its evidence names, a
#>     listing of its zone plus up to three existing examples there, and the doc it quotes
#>     (SKILL.md phase 7). That is where CONTEXT_PATHS, the conventions file and the output example
#>     come from. The name of the catalogue row is the skill's name (blueprint.md §1.3); a skill
#>     that is the entry point of a pair names its subagent in the step that delegates, and says
#>     what the subagent may not do (write, in a read-only pair).
name: {{SKILL_NAME}}
#> description — the only line the agent reads when deciding whether to load this skill, so it
#> decides whether the skill ever fires. Write it in third person, one paragraph, under 1024
#> characters, covering three things in this order:
#>   (a) what the skill does, concretely, naming the real paths or commands it touches;
#>   (b) the literal trigger phrases a developer types for it — lift the wording from the
#>       request_shapes examples in signals.json rather than paraphrasing;
#>   (c) a "Not for ..." clause naming the nearest neighbour it must not steal work from.
#> No first person, no "this skill", no "helps you", no marketing adjectives.
#> Shape to copy: "Adds a REST endpoint under src/api/ with a zod schema, handler, route
#> registration, and a Vitest suite. Use when the user says 'add an endpoint', 'new route',
#> 'expose X over the API', or 'wire up an API for'. Not for changing an existing endpoint's
#> business logic, and not for GraphQL resolvers."
description: {{SKILL_DESCRIPTION}}
agentify-id: {{AGENTIFY_ID}}
agentify-version: 1
agentify-generated: {{GENERATED_DATE}}
agentify-evidence: {{EVIDENCE_LINE}}
# Safe to delete or edit. This work was brought to you by Agentic Studio.
